  "estimatedMinutes": 1,
  "statement": "The limiting sum of the infinite series \\[ \\frac1{10}+\\frac2{10^2}+\\frac3{10^3}+\\cdots, \\] whose \\(n\\)th term is \\(\\frac{n}{10^n},\\) is:",
  "answerChoices": [
    {
      "label": "A",
      "text": "\\(\\dfrac19\\)"
    },
    {
      "label": "B",
      "text": "\\(\\dfrac{10}{81}\\)"
    },
    {
      "label": "C",
      "text": "\\(\\dfrac18\\)"
    },
    {
      "label": "D",
      "text": "\\(\\dfrac{17}{72}\\)"
    },
    {
      "label": "E",
      "text": "larger than any finite quantity"
    }
  ],
  "answer": {
    "letter": "b",
    "value": "10/81"
  },
  "solutions": [
    {
      "id": "sol.ahsme-1962-p40.live",
      "kind": "proof-sketch",
      "text": "For \\(|x|\\lt1,\\) \\[ \\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty}nx^n=\\frac{x}{(1-x)^2}. \\] Taking \\(x=\\frac{1}{10}\\) gives \\[ \\frac{\\frac{1}{10}}{(\\frac{9}{10})^2}=\\frac{10}{81}. \\]\n\nThus, the correct answer is B.",
      "hints": [
        "Start from \\(\\sum_{n=0}^{\\infty}x^n=\\frac{1}{1-x}\\)",
        "Differentiate and then multiply by \\(x\\)"
      ],
      "images": []
    }
  ],
